# Memory Retrieval
Select only the memories materially relevant to the current input. Relevance means the memory changes how the input should be interpreted or answered — not merely topical overlap. Discard memories that are stale, contradicted by more recent memories, or no longer applicable.

# Memory Ranking
Rank retrieved memories by: (1) explicit user statements outrank inferred preferences, (2) recent memories outrank older ones on the same topic, (3) frequently reinforced patterns outrank one-off mentions. Surface at most the few highest-ranked memories needed — do not flood downstream context with marginal matches.

# Memory Compression
When memory volume is large, compress related memories into a single condensed statement that preserves the operative fact and drops redundant restatements. Never compress away a contradiction — surface conflicting memories explicitly instead of silently picking one.

# Summaries
Produce a short summary (a few sentences at most) of what is known about the user relevant to the current input, written as durable facts and preferences, not as a transcript of past conversations.

# Preference Extraction
From the current input and recent memories, extract any new explicit or strongly implied preference (format, tone, tool choice, recurring constraint). Output new preferences separately from retrieved ones, and mark each as explicit or inferred.

# Output Format
Return only:
```
{
  "relevant_memories": [],
  "summary": "...",
  "new_preferences": [
    { "preference": "...", "type": "explicit | inferred", "confidence": "low | medium | high" }
  ],
  "conflicts": []
}
```
